          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Recurrent urinary tract infections (UTIs) are a common issue, particularly if you have had three infections within a year. Guidance indicates that recurrent infections should be evaluated, and urine cultures can be utilized to identify any underlying complications or resistance patterns that may require different management.\n\nA UTI is primarily a bacterial infection affecting the urinary system, mostly involving the bladder and urethra. There are two types of infections: uncomplicated and complicated. Uncomplicated infections occur in people with normal urinary tracts and immune systems, while complicated infections might involve structural abnormalities, pregnancy, or other health issues (source: condition-urinary-tract-infection).\n\nFor managing uncomplicated UTIs, short-course antibiotic therapy is typically preferred, using agents that are effective in reaching the urine while minimizing effects on gut bacteria. First-line options usually include specific antibiotics, but the choice can depend on local resistance patterns (source: guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment). \n\nIt's advisable to discuss your recurrent infections with a clinician. They may assess potential underlying causes and consider additional strategies to prevent recurrence, such as lifestyle changes or further diagnostic evaluations.\n\nI could not cover specific medication names, doses, or detailed treatment plans. Always consult a healthcare professional for personalized medical advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
